    "narrative": "The Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup is anchored today by **Corbin Carroll**, whose 87.14 efficiency score against Ryan Feltner makes him our premier start. **Corbin Carroll** benefits immensely from a porous 4.96 xERA matchup and a prime #2 slot, providing the necessary high-end production for our stolen base-starved roster. Following closely in the rankings are **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) and **Gabriel Moreno** (71.10), both of whom take advantage of exploitable pitching profiles. **Mickey Moniak** is particularly intriguing, with his score surged by a +14% platoon advantage against Merrill Kelly, while **Gabriel Moreno** continues to maintain strong recency metrics that keep his floor safely above the 70-point threshold despite the uncertainty of a pending lineup.\n\nWe are navigating a few tactical tightropes, most notably with **Zach McKinstry**, who remains in the lineup despite a marginal 47.30 score. While his raw projections are modest, the optimizer holds him for his efficiency against Foster Griffin; his +6% platoon edge and the rare +2% boost provided by the opposing pitcher's slow delivery profile offer a sneaky, high-upside opportunity to chase steals in a bottom-of-the-order role. Conversely, our toughest bench decision involved **Max Muncy**, who finished with a 40.98 score. **Max Muncy** was narrowly edged out by **Travis Bazzana** and **Cole Young** because the optimizer prioritized their defensive slot flexibility and specific positional scarcity over the limited power ceiling **Max Muncy** possesses against a difficult arm like Drew Rasmussen. \n\nRisk management remains a priority given that many of these projections rely on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions. **Austin Riley** is currently in the lineup with a 59.34 score, but he carries a warning due to his assumed #8 spot in the order, which acts as a drag on his overall output; if he slides any further down the order, he risks dropping below our 40-point threshold. We are also monitoring **Tyler Soderstrom**, who faces a stiff test against the 3.17 xERA of Drew Rasmussen. While his 57.87 score is respectable, his -19% SP skill penalty highlights the volatility inherent in his matchup, necessitating a close watch as official lineups are posted throughout the afternoon.",
